25
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,720
In another word which is used in Scrum is empiricism.

26
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:13,490
Empiricism in Scrum Guide, the definition is process control type in which only the past is accepted

27
00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:20,300
as certain and in which decisions are based on observation, experience and experimentation.

28
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:24,240
Empiricism has three pillars.

29
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:29,310
They are transparency, inspection and adaptation.

30
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:36,590
Now, before we look at the words, let's look at what empiricism is generally.

31
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:46,520
So in simple words, empiricism is the belief that people should rely on practical experience and experiments

32
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:48,620
as the basis of knowledge.

33
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:01,000
So we take as knowledge something that we have tried and we know that works.

34
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:04,780
Not from theory, but from practice.

35
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:06,010
We have tried it.

36
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:07,630
We know that it works.

37
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:14,950
And going back here now, a couple of words here

38
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:23,020
at the end of this definition, empiricism has three pillars, and pillar means a fundamental principle.

39
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:26,140
So it has three principles.

40
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:35,740
Transparency and transparent means able to be seen through or open or easy to understand.

41
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:41,620
So what we know is easy to understand, easy to explain.

42
00:03:42,490 --> 00:03:46,420
Then inspection is looking at something carefully.

43
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:55,870
So we rely on observation, looking at something carefully, and taking knowledge from from that inspection

44
00:03:56,260 --> 00:04:00,850
and adaptation, changing to fit some goal or situation.

45
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:07,900
So we take the knowledge and then we adapt ourselves.

46
00:04:08,890 --> 00:04:13,210
For further work based on that knowledge that we have.

47
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:21,410
Now we go on and the next word is forecast of functionality.

48
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:24,470
In Scrum Guide

49
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:32,900
it says that for cost is the selection of items on the product backlog that developers deem feasible

50
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:35,390
for implementation in a Sprint.

51
00:04:36,530 --> 00:04:39,440
Now forecast is a prediction.

52
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:42,800
For example, we often speak about a weather forecast.

53
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:50,060
So prediction of weather conditions and to deem means to consider or to think.

54
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:57,200
And feasible means possible to do.

55
00:04:57,650 --> 00:05:05,630
Now let's read the definition once again. Forecast of functionality or some kind of a prediction of functionality

56
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:16,580
is the selection of items from the product backlog that developers think is possible for implementation

57
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:17,600
in a sprint.

58
00:05:18,950 --> 00:05:19,170
Okay.

59
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:27,830
I think this is more, this is easier to understand when we translate those words from more advanced to

60
00:05:27,830 --> 00:05:29,840
more simple and understandable.

61
00:05:31,550 --> 00:05:32,990
Well, let's move on.

62
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:35,020
The next one is increment.

63
00:05:35,230 --> 00:05:36,050
Increment.

64
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:45,500
In Scrum Guide, the definition is increment is Scrum artifact that defines the complete and valuable

65
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:49,130
work produced by the developers during the Sprint.

66
00:05:50,390 --> 00:05:55,310
The sum of all increments for the final product.

67
00:05:56,390 --> 00:06:00,320
So an increment in simple language is a part.

68
00:06:01,150 --> 00:06:03,840
A part of the whole software.

69
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:06,740
And valuable.

70
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:12,890
Valuable work is something that has monetary or other values, something that has value.

71
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:18,500
Now the next word is Product Backlog.

72
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:24,910
This is maybe pretty easy, but again, let's look at it.

73
00:06:25,390 --> 00:06:34,630
This is a Scrum artifact that consists of an ordered list of the work to be done in order to create,

74
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:37,690
maintain and sustain a product.

75
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:41,960
Now, an interesting word here is to sustain, what does it mean?

76
00:06:42,070 --> 00:06:46,480
Sustain means to support, to provide everything needed.

77
00:06:47,930 --> 00:06:51,200
Moving on Product Backlog Refinement.

78
00:06:52,320 --> 00:07:02,280
The activity in a Sprint through which the Product Owner and the Developers add granularity to the Product

79
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:03,090
Backlog.

80
00:07:03,890 --> 00:07:11,180
Now the word refinement. Refinement is small changes that improve something.

81
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:15,260
So we add small changes to improve something.

82
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:18,110
And in this case, what are those changes?

83
00:07:18,110 --> 00:07:20,690
It's adding granularity.

84
00:07:20,750 --> 00:07:25,010
Granularity means including a lot of small details.

85
00:07:25,010 --> 00:07:29,570
So what do we do is we take one Backlog item.

86
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:37,580
For example, we need to create a web page and then we add granularity to this task.

87
00:07:37,580 --> 00:07:45,350
It means we say, okay, for this web page, we need to create this menu and this menu and the panel

88
00:07:45,350 --> 00:07:48,500
somewhere in some buttons and and all of these things.

89
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:54,050
Or we need to create a design for that web page first.

90
00:07:54,050 --> 00:07:54,280
Yeah.

91
00:07:54,320 --> 00:08:03,410
So all of those details is actually granularity because we take one big task and we break it into smaller

92
00:08:03,410 --> 00:08:07,550
tasks and it means we added granularity to this task.

93
00:08:09,570 --> 00:08:13,990
Okay, going on. Scrum Board.

94
00:08:13,990 --> 00:08:25,400
A Scrum Board is a physical board to visualize information for and by the Scrum team, often used to manage

95
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:26,900
Sprint Backlog.

96
00:08:27,830 --> 00:08:34,130
Scrum Boards are optional implementation within Scrum to make information visible.

97
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:38,039
I think you all know what a Scrum Board is.

98
00:08:38,039 --> 00:08:39,720
If you don't know, you can just Google it.

99
00:08:39,750 --> 00:08:46,860
Again, it's a physical board where you take the stickers and you stick them to the board and all of

100
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:55,010
those stickers, they have some tusks written on them and we move the stickers from the Product Backlog

101
00:08:55,050 --> 00:09:02,310
to the the next column, which is like In progress, something that we're working on,

102
00:09:02,310 --> 00:09:08,880
and when it's done we move it to the Done part of the board. The word visualize.

103
00:09:09,970 --> 00:09:10,300
Again

104
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:13,660
pay attention to the pronunciation here.

105
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:18,190
Visualize is to make visible or to make seen.

106
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:27,190
So we do all of that in order to look at the board and quickly see how is the work going on.

107
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:29,740
And optional.

108
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:30,970
It's an optional

109
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:33,700
implementation means not compulsory.

110
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:34,960
It's not a must.

111
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:41,020
The Scrum team can decide if they want to Scrum Board or if they don't want a Scrum Board.

112
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:49,950
Now the teams, in the Scrum we often say that the team is self-managing.

113
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:51,090
What does it mean?

114
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:59,080
Scrum teams are cross-functional, meaning the members have all the skills necessary to create value

115
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:08,490
each Sprint. They're also self-managing, meaning they internally decide who does what, when and how.

116
00:10:09,590 --> 00:10:14,420
So self managing basically means that they manage themselves.

117
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:18,920
There is no boss who tells them what to do.

118
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:27,560
They decide in a team what they are going to do, when and how and who is going to do it.

119
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:32,570
And an interesting word here is to decide internally.

120
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:39,710
Internally, this word means, an internal means that refers to activities done on the inside.

121
00:10:39,710 --> 00:10:47,630
So not somebody from the outside tells the team what to do, but the team decides internally among themselves,

122
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:49,700
themselves, what to do.

123
00:10:52,730 --> 00:10:53,690
Okay, the next

124
00:10:54,290 --> 00:10:56,870
the next one is Technical Debt.

125
00:10:57,230 --> 00:10:57,800
Debt.

126
00:10:57,830 --> 00:10:59,710
Pay attention to the pronunciation

127
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:04,400
here, we see b, but we don't read b,  oaky, we see debt.

128
00:11:05,450 --> 00:11:15,560
The Scrum definition is, Technical Debt is is the typically unpredictable overhead of maintaining the

129
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:23,390
product, often caused by less than ideal design decisions contributing to the total cost of ownership.

130
00:11:24,850 --> 00:11:30,790
Now a debt is something that you owe, something that you need to pay back or give back.

131
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:41,510
And an overhead means additional work or additional cost, which does not directly contribute to creating

132
00:11:41,530 --> 00:11:42,340
product.

133
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:47,260
And here we say unpredictable overhead of maintaining product.

134
00:11:47,260 --> 00:11:54,250
So what we do it all has the direct connection to the final product.

135
00:11:54,250 --> 00:11:59,740
But this overhead is something that does not have this direct connection to the product.

136
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:09,220
It may be some other activity that we do in the team in order to make the team function or the organization

137
00:12:09,490 --> 00:12:10,090
function.

138
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:15,910
But it's not directly connected to like coding and testing in the actual product.

139
00:12:18,970 --> 00:12:23,650
And the last word in this lesson is velocity.

140
00:12:24,130 --> 00:12:34,420
So in Scrum velocity - an optional but often used indication of the amount of Product Backlog turned

141
00:12:34,420 --> 00:12:43,090
into an incremental product during a Sprint by a Scrum team, it is tracked by the Developers for use

142
00:12:43,090 --> 00:12:47,980
within the Scrum team. So we concentrate on the first part.

143
00:12:47,980 --> 00:12:57,850
Velocity is indication of the amount of Product Backlog items which return into the final Increment

144
00:12:57,850 --> 00:13:00,790
for the Sprint. And velocity

145
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:07,930
in simple words means speed or rate at which something is happening.

